readme_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum — regime-shift robustness, migration continuity, and world-transition survival truth after rev0472\n\nThis tranche locks the next seam around **whether a state that survives ordinary churn can also survive named world transitions without silently losing the governing world that made the sentence true**.\nThe key decisions now made explicit in the archive are:\n\n- **steady-state durability is a first-class rung, but it is weaker than regime-shift robustness across named transition classes**\n- **durable in current world only, upgrade-path conditional, same-user-and-storage dependent, service-account-fork exposed, config-mode override exposed, product-family transition blocked, named transition survived for named slice only, and broader all-transition robustness blocked are different truths**\n- **ordinary restart is weaker than same-world migration, same-world migration is weaker than named transition survival, and named transition survival is weaker than broader transition-set robustness**\n- **bytes remaining on disk, service startup, a new WebUI, copied instances, and apparent post-update calm can no longer silently impersonate world continuity**\n- **every serious regime-shift sentence now needs one receipt that preserves transition class, principal continuity, identity continuity, storage-root continuity, control-surface continuity, clone-or-collision warning state, highest honest transition-safe sentence, and the blocked stronger sentence**\n\nNew docs added in this tranche:\n\n- `1978-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-robustness-migration-continuity-and-world-transition-fragmentation-evaluation.md`\n- `1979-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-contract-sheet-page-transition-class-world-continuity-and-survival-budget-interface-spec.md`\n- `1980-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-review-page-will-the-durable-state-survive-upgrades-service-user-changes-storage-relocation-and-install-mode-flips-interface-spec.md`\n- `1981-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-proof-page-transition-matrix-world-identity-continuity-and-robustness-ceiling-interface-spec.md`\n- `1982-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-timeline-page-upgrade-migrate-rebind-relocate-fork-and-recertify-events-interface-spec.md`\n- `1983-remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift-lineage-receipt-page-transition-scope-world-continuity-and-blocked-stronger-sentences-interface-spec.md`\n\nWhat this tranche contributes to the larger doctrine:\n\n- `true through ordinary churn` can no longer hide whether upgrades, account changes, storage relocation, or install-style changes preserve the same governing world\n- surviving bytes can no longer silently impersonate surviving shares configuration, identity, or control-surface continuity\n- unsupported clone-like shortcuts can no longer masquerade as supported migration proof\n- later operators can open one receipt and see exactly which transition classes are in scope, which prerequisites preserve continuity, which steps forced rebind or reconnect, and which stronger universal-migration sentence the product refused to make\n'
status_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum — regime-shift robustness, migration continuity, and world-transition survival truth after rev0472\n\nThe next seam after **postcondition durability across ordinary churn** is now explicit:\n\n- the archive can already say whether the intended state survives reconnects, future arrivals, hidden returners, replay risk, restore fragility, and path churn across a named steady-state horizon\n- it still needed to own the harder truth of **whether that same state survives upgrades, reinstalls, service-account changes, storage relocation, config-mode flips, install-style changes, and other named world transitions without silently becoming a different governing world**\n- current Resilio docs reinforce that gap because v3 update guidance depends on install style, same command line parameters, same user, and same storage path; service migration can either preserve shares or require re-share and reconnect; switching service account can create a different storage world with no old folders present; config mode can disable or override prior WebUI-managed shares; Linux can create storage in the current directory or run multiple manual instances; and cloning the instance is explicitly unsupported\n\nThis pass turns that gap into a first-class product object: **the remedy-hardening-attestation regime-shift robustness, migration continuity, and world-transition survival case**.\n\nWhat is newly true in the archive:\n\n- **steady-state durability is weaker than regime-shift robustness across named transition classes**\n- **durable in current world only, upgrade-path conditional, service-account fork risk open, config-mode override risk open, version-family transition blocked, unsupported clone-derived transition, named transition survived for named slice only, and broader all-transition robustness blocked are separate public truths**\n- **ordinary restart is weaker than same-world migration, same-world migration is weaker than named transition survival, and named transition survival is weaker than broader transition-set robustness**\n- **surviving bytes, service startup, a new WebUI, copied instances, and apparent post-update calm now degrade into evidence inputs instead of impersonating a world-continuity verdict**\n- **every serious regime-shift sentence now needs one receipt that preserves transition class, principal continuity, identity continuity, storage-root continuity, control-surface continuity, clone-or-collision warning state, highest honest transition-safe sentence, and the blocked stronger sentence**\n\nWhat remains intentionally true:\n\n- durability across ordinary churn still matters\n- recurrence risk still matters\n- steady-state horizons still matter\n- but none of those may substitute for one explicit answer about whether the named regime shift preserves the same governing world strongly enough to keep saying the same thing afterward\n'
eval_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0472 — why regime-shift robustness, migration continuity, and world-transition survival now sit on the non-clone side\n\nCurrent official Resilio docs are still admirably candid that `the state survives ordinary churn in this world` and `the same state survives a named world transition while preserving the same governing world` are not one flat truth.\n`Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3` still says update process depends on installation style, and non-default CLI launches must restart with the same command line parameters and same user so Sync points to the same storage folder and preserves configuration.\nThe same v3 update guide still says Resilio Sync Business cannot be updated to v3 and may lose shares configuration even while files stay on the device.\n`FAQ Resilio Sync 3.0.0` still says v2 and v3 preserve synchronization compatibility, while linked devices under one identity should all be updated to v3 to avoid license conflicts.\n`Running Sync as a service on Windows` still says service installation can migrate settings or do a clean installation, and clean installation requires re-sharing folders and connecting them to already existing peers.\n`Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` still says switching the service account to Local System creates a different storage folder and that seeing no old added folders afterward is expected.\n`Sync Storage folder` still says storage paths materially differ by current user, Local Service, Local System, Linux launch directory, config-defined storage path, package install style, and NAS platform.\n`Running Sync in configuration mode` still says config mode can define a non-default storage path, can set up only Standard folders, and that shared folders in config disable WebUI while overriding folders previously added from WebUI.\n`Guide to Linux, and Sync peculiarities` still says Linux can run multiple instances if the operator manually assigns ports and storage, and otherwise creates storage in the current directory.\n`Cloning Sync` still says cloning any Sync instance is unsupported and may produce multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n`Resilio Sync 3.0 change log` still records upgrade-era UI and warning fixes, reinforcing that visible calm and transition-safe continuity are not the same sentence.\n\nThis is strong operational candor.\nIt is also exactly why AnonSync should not clone the present contract.\nOne ordinary answer to `will this durable state survive the named regime shift while preserving the same governing identity, storage world, control surface, and share continuity?` still depends on combining install-style branching, same-parameter launch discipline, version-family caveats, service-account forks, storage-root location, config-mode overrides, Linux multi-instance discipline, explicit anti-cloning guidance, and current changelog memory.\n\nSo this tranche freezes a stronger replacement line: **durable in current world only, supported-path conditional, same-user-and-storage dependent, service-world fork exposed, config-mode override exposed, unsupported clone-derived transition, named transition survived, and broader all-transition robustness become separate modeled truths.**\n\nThat is why this revision adds five more first-class pages: **Remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift contract sheet**, **regime-shift review**, **regime-shift proof**, **regime-shift timeline**, and **regime-shift lineage receipt**.\n'
sources_add = "\n## rev0473 source set — regime-shift robustness, migration continuity, and world-transition survival\n\nThe most load-bearing source set for this pass was:\n\n- Resilio's current `Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3` article, which still says update process depends on installation style, that non-default CLI launches must restart with the same command and same parameters so Sync points to the same storage folder, and that Resilio Sync Business cannot be updated to v3 without risk of losing shares configuration.\n- Resilio's current `FAQ Resilio Sync 3.0.0` article, which still says v2 and v3 preserve synchronization compatibility, while linked devices under one identity should all be updated to v3 to avoid license conflicts.\n- Resilio's current `Running Sync as a service on Windows` article, which still says service install has both a migrate-settings path and a clean-install path, and that clean installation requires re-sharing and reconnecting folders.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` article, which still says switching the service account to Local System creates a different storage world where old added folders are absent and a different storage path applies.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Storage folder` article, which still says storage locations differ materially by current user, Local Service, Local System, Linux launch directory, config-defined storage path, package install style, and NAS platform.\n- Resilio's current `Running Sync in configuration mode` article, which still says config mode can define a non-default storage path, can set up only Standard folders, and that shared folders in config disable WebUI while overriding folders previously added from WebUI.\n- Resilio's current `Guide to Linux, and Sync peculiarities` article, which still says Linux can run multiple instances when the operator manually assigns ports and storage, and that default storage is otherwise created in the current directory.\n- Resilio's current `Cloning Sync` article, which still says cloning a Sync instance is unsupported and may yield instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n- Resilio's current `Resilio Sync 3.0 change log`, which still records upgrade-era UI and warning fixes that remind us visible post-transition calm is not a continuity receipt.\n\nThose sources were enough to tighten the line again:\n\n- current Resilio still deserves credit for candid migration ingredients\n- but current Resilio still answers `does the durable state survive this named world transition while preserving the same governing world?` too diffusely\n- AnonSync should therefore prefer explicit remedy-hardening-attestation-regime-shift sheets, reviews, proofs, timelines, and lineage receipts over overloaded update notes, service-install branching, storage-root conventions, config-mode caveats, Linux launch discipline, and anti-cloning warnings\n\nPrimary sources:\n\n- Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/31194252837779-Updating-installation-to-Resilio-Sync-v3\n\n- FAQ Resilio Sync 3.0.0\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/32109883606035-FAQ-Resilio-Sync-3-0-0\n\n- Running Sync as a service on Windows\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207701296-Running-Sync-as-a-service-on-Windows\n\n- Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207724196-Sync-Service-Troubleshooting-on-Windows\n\n- Sync Storage folder\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206664690-Sync-Storage-folder\n\n- Running Sync in configuration mode\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206178884-Running-Sync-in-configuration-mode\n\n- Guide to Linux, and Sync peculiarities\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204762449-Guide-to-Linux-and-Sync-peculiarities\n\n- Cloning Sync\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/360018421940-Cloning-Sync\n\n- Resilio Sync 3.0 change log\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/31386579044755-Resilio-Sync-3-0-change-log\n"

